.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt INTEL_RAPL 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm intel_rapl
.Nd Intel RAPL package power limits and power measurement
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following line in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device intel_rapl"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
intel_rapl_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver gives access to the package domain of the Running Average Power
Limit (RAPL) interface of Intel processors from Sandy Bridge onwards.
It reports how much power each processor package draws and lets the
administrator set the package power limits that the processor enforces
by adjusting its own frequency.
.Pp
RAPL has two package limits.
PL1 is the sustained limit: the processor keeps its average power at or
below PL1 over the PL1 time window.
PL2 is the burst limit, enforced over a much shorter window.
Lowering PL1 trades performance for lower temperature and fan noise, which
is useful on a laptop whose cooling cannot sustain the default limits.
.Pp
One
.Nm
device attaches to the first CPU of each processor package.
All register accesses for a package are made on that CPU.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following variables are available for each package under
.Va dev.intel_rapl.N ,
where
.Va N
is the device unit.
Power is in milliwatts, energy in microjoules and time in microseconds.
.Bl -tag -width "hw_max_window_us"
.It Va power_mw
Average package power over the last second.
.It Va energy_uj
Package energy consumed since the driver attached.
.It Va pl1_mw , pl2_mw
The sustained and burst power limits.
Writing a value also enables the limit.
The value is rounded to the nearest step the hardware supports, usually
125 mW.
.It Va pl1_enabled , pl2_enabled
Whether the processor enforces the limit.
.It Va pl1_window_us , pl2_window_us
The time windows of the limits.
The hardware supports only a sparse set of windows; a written value is
rounded to the nearest one.
A PL2 window is typically a few milliseconds and a PL1 window tens of
seconds.
Many client processors ignore the PL2 window, and writing it then fails
with
.Er EPERM .
.It Va min_allowed_mw , max_allowed_mw
The range of limits the driver accepts.
They are set from the hardware's reported minimum and maximum, or to
5000 mW and twice the thermal design power when it does not report them,
and are then widened to include the limits set by the firmware.
A
.Va max_allowed_mw
of 0 removes the upper bound.
Limits that do not fit the register are always refused.
.It Va tdp_mw
The thermal design power reported by the hardware.
.It Va hw_min_mw , hw_max_mw , hw_max_window_us
The minimum and maximum power and the longest time window reported by
the hardware, or 0 if it does not report them.
.It Va locked
Set when the firmware has locked the power limit register.
Writes to the limits then fail with
.Er EPERM
until the next reset.
.It Va package , cpu
The package number and the CPU used to access its registers.
.El
.Pp
The writable variables can only be changed while the system security
level is 0 or lower; see
.Xr security 7 .
Settings in
.Xr sysctl.conf 5
are applied before the security level is raised at boot.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Limit the sustained power of the first package to 15 W, averaged over 28
seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl dev.intel_rapl.0.pl1_mw=15000
sysctl dev.intel_rapl.0.pl1_window_us=28000000
.Ed
.Pp
The same settings in
.Xr sysctl.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dev.intel_rapl.0.pl1_mw=15000
dev.intel_rapl.0.pl1_window_us=28000000
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Writing a limit fails with
.Er EINVAL
when the value is outside the accepted range,
and with
.Er EPERM
when the register is locked or the processor ignored the write.
In the second case the previous register value is put back.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr cpufreq 4 ,
.Xr hwpstate_intel 4 ,
.Xr sysctl.conf 5 ,
.Xr security 7 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Mx 4.2 .
It is based on the
.Nm
kernel module by
.An Rafal Rabczuk .
.Sh CAVEATS
Many laptops also set package power limits through a memory-mapped copy
of these registers or through the ACPI Dynamic Platform and Thermal
Framework.
The processor enforces the lowest of the limits, so raising a limit with
.Nm
may have no effect, and firmware may restore its own limits later, for
example when the power source changes.
.Pp
A limit set too low can make the system unresponsive.
.Sh BUGS
Only the package domain is supported; the core, graphics, DRAM and
platform domains are not.
.Pp
Silvermont and Airmont Atom processors use different units and are not
supported.
